#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Paper text: ML in battery management — blocks consumed by build_docx.py.
Citations use [[doi]] placeholders resolved to numbered [n] at build time."""

TITLE = "Machine learning in battery management systems: a structured scoping review and classification of 103 peer-reviewed studies, 2019\u20132026"

AUTHOR_LINES = [("Hussain Touhid Siddiquee", "Leading University, Sylhet, Bangladesh"),
                ("Syeda Salsabil Islam Ariya", "Leading University, Sylhet, Bangladesh"),
                ("Jasimul Islam Chowdhury", "Leading University, Sylhet, Bangladesh")]

ABSTRACT = [
 "Machine learning (ML) has become a load-bearing component of modern battery management systems (BMS). This scoping review classifies 103 peer-reviewed journal articles (2019\u20132026) on machine learning for battery management \u2014 drawn from a Crossref-verified, rule-based eligible pool of 799 records \u2014 uniformly by management function, method family, chemistry, data source, validation practice.",

 "Within the corpus, method families stratify by period: classical shallow learners contribute 12 of the 23 papers published 2019\u20132021, deep sequence models dominate from 2022 onward, physics-informed and hybrid approaches reach 24 of 103 papers (22 primary), and the first large-language-model studies appear in 2025\u20132026. Health estimation and prognostics form the largest application cluster (35 papers), followed by the cross-cutting cluster (24), state estimation (17), fault and safety (9), charging control (8), thermal management (8), and cell balancing (2). Because the corpus was curated under function-and-year quotas, these distributions describe the sample rather than the field; function-level baselines from the quota-free eligible pool (query-theme tags validated against the classified subset) are reported alongside.",

 "Three structural weaknesses emerge. First, validation practice is heterogeneous and often weak: only 8 of the 88 primary-application studies demonstrate their estimators online or on real vehicle or fleet data, while 17 rely on within-cell or k-fold splits that overstate generalization. Second, benchmark-data dependence is high but narrow: the Severson/Toyota, NASA, and CALCE datasets recur across ostensibly independent studies. Third, laboratory-condition accuracy claims are rarely tested against chemistry, temperature, and current-profile shifts \u2014 the field\u2019s defining laboratory-to-field gap. Physics-informed architectures, often assumed more rigorous by construction, are not: neither stringency contrast with data-driven peers is statistically significant, and a label-noise sensitivity analysis brackets the strong-protocol share between 40% and 59%. The review closes with a research agenda prioritizing cross-domain validation standards, uncertainty-aware models, edge-deployable architectures, and the safe integration of foundation models into safety-critical BMS software.",
]

KEYWORDS = ("machine learning; battery management systems; lithium-ion batteries; state of charge; "
            "state of health; remaining useful life; deep learning; physics-informed neural networks; "
            "transfer learning; battery safety")

BLOCKS = [
('h1', '1. Introduction'),

('h2', '1.1 Electrification and the battery management problem'),
('p',
 "Lithium-ion batteries now anchor three converging transitions: the electrification of road transport, the proliferation of stationary storage in renewable-heavy grids, and the electrification of aerospace, marine, and industrial systems. In each of these settings, the battery is not a passive component but an actively managed asset whose usable capacity, lifetime, and safety depend on operating decisions made in real time. The battery management system is the embedded cyber-physical layer that makes those decisions: it senses cell voltages, currents, and temperatures; estimates unobservable internal states; protects against over-charge, over-discharge, and thermal abuse; balances cells; and increasingly negotiates charging strategies with the surrounding power system [[10.1016/j.egyai.2025.100585]]."),

('p',
 "The difficulty of the management problem stems from the fact that the quantities that matter most \u2014 state of charge (SOC), state of health (SOH), internal temperature, and the onset of degradation or fault mechanisms \u2014 are not directly measurable. They must be inferred from noisy, partially observable terminal measurements under widely varying operating conditions. Model-based approaches built on equivalent-circuit or electrochemical models have served the industry well, but they face a persistent trade-off between fidelity and computational tractability, and their parameters drift as cells age [[10.1016/j.joule.2019.11.018]]. It is in this inference-and-decision layer that machine learning has made its most forceful entry."),

('h2', '1.2 The rise of machine learning in battery management'),
('p',
 "Machine learning entered battery management through estimation. Early applications treated SOC and SOH estimation as supervised regression on hand-engineered features, using support vector machines, Gaussian process regression, and shallow neural networks. Two studies then reset expectations for the whole field. Severson and colleagues showed that the entire cycle life of a lithium-ion cell could be predicted with a median test error near 9% using only features extracted from the first hundred cycles, on a public dataset of 124 cells [[10.1038/s41560-019-0356-8]]. Attia and colleagues demonstrated closed-loop Bayesian optimization of fast-charging protocols, identifying high-cycle-life protocols among 224 candidates in 16 days of closed-loop cycling rather than the more than 500 days an exhaustive search without early prediction would take [[10.1038/s41586-020-1994-5]]. Together these results reframed battery management as a data problem in which learning systems could outperform hand-tuned models both in estimation accuracy and in experiment efficiency."),

('p',
 "The five years that followed produced a wave of published applications across every BMS function: recurrent and convolutional networks for SOC and SOH [[10.1016/j.energy.2020.117664]][[10.1016/j.energy.2023.126855]], graph and transformer architectures for prognostics [[10.1016/j.ress.2022.108947]][[10.1016/j.energy.2024.133418]], physics-informed neural networks that embed electrochemical and thermal priors into learning systems [[10.1016/j.jechem.2022.11.036]][[10.1109/jrfid.2022.3211841]], transfer learning schemes that port estimators across chemistries and vehicles [[10.1109/tpel.2021.3117788]][[10.1016/j.energy.2023.127033]], reinforcement learning agents for charging and prognostic model selection [[10.1016/j.ress.2025.111392]], and, most recently, large language model (LLM) agents for fleet-scale risk analysis and vehicle-cloud battery management [[10.1016/j.ress.2026.112416]][[10.1016/j.ensm.2026.104983]]."),

('h2', '1.3 Existing reviews and the literature gap'),
('p',
 "The review literature has grown alongside the applications, but it is fragmented along the same axes as the primary studies. An early perspective on data-driven SOC and SOH prediction [[10.1038/s42256-020-0156-7]] set the agenda; function-specific reviews cover SOC estimation [[10.1016/j.est.2022.104664]][[10.20517/jmi.2024.84]], SOH estimation [[10.1016/j.apenergy.2021.117346]], and prognostics [[10.1016/j.jechem.2023.03.026]] in depth, while method-specific reviews follow individual threads such as deep-learning state estimation [[10.1016/j.egyai.2025.100585]], physics-informed learning for impedance-based diagnostics [[10.1016/j.rser.2023.113807]], or interpretable ML for prognosis [[10.1002/aenm.202503067]]. Domain-anchored reviews examine fault diagnosis and safety [[10.3390/electronics10111309]][[10.1016/j.etran.2023.100254]] and thermal management [[10.1016/j.est.2023.106688]], alongside broader degradation-method surveys [[10.3390/batteries10070220]]. What is largely missing is a cross-functional, quantitative account of the field\u2019s trajectory: which method families are actually being used for which management tasks, on which data, validated how, and how that picture has shifted over the last five years."),

('p',
 "This gap matters because the field\u2019s self-image drives its research allocation. If, for example, most published health estimators are validated on within-cell splits of public datasets, the community\u2019s sense of progress is calibrated to a benchmark regime that differs sharply from fleet deployment. If interpretability claims are concentrated in a small method family, the safety case for certification-grade ML in production BMS is weaker than the volume of publications suggests. Answering such questions requires a corpus-level, cross-functional account of the field rather than another narrative synthesis of one function."),

('h2', '1.4 Contribution of this paper'),
('p',
 "This paper makes four contributions. First, it assembles a verified corpus of 103 peer-reviewed journal articles (2019\u20132026) on machine learning for battery management, drawn from a rule-based eligible pool of 799 Crossref-verified records, in which every reference\u2019s bibliographic metadata was confirmed against the Crossref registry by direct DOI resolution. Second, it develops a two-axis classification framework \u2014 BMS function \u00d7 method family \u2014 extended with chemistry, data-source, and validation-practice fields, and applies it uniformly to all 103 papers. Third, it quantifies the corpus\u2019s composition across functions, methods, data, and validation (Section 3), identifying the within-corpus shift from classical learners through deep sequence models to physics-informed hybrids, optimization-augmented learning, and early foundation-model applications, and anchoring function-level baselines on the quota-free eligible pool. Fourth, it provides a critical, function-by-function review of what the corpus actually demonstrates (Sections 4\u20138) and distills a gap-driven research agenda (Section 9)."),

('p',
 "Fig. 1 previews the analysis, mapping the entire corpus across management functions and method families: the field\u2019s center of gravity sits squarely in the health-prognostics complex, hybrid model\u2013data approaches now span every function, and learning-based charging control remains a concentrated Bayesian-optimization stronghold. The remainder of the paper is organized as follows. Section 2 details the review methodology: search strategy, screening procedure, inclusion and exclusion criteria, and the classification framework, together with its limitations. Section 3 presents the corpus overview and five-year bibliometric analysis. Sections 4 through 7 review machine learning for state estimation, health estimation and prognostics, charging control, and thermal management, fault detection, and safety, respectively. Section 8 examines cross-cutting directions \u2014 physics-informed learning, interpretability and uncertainty, digital twins and cloud BMS, and foundation models. Section 9 formulates research gaps and future directions, and Section 10 concludes."),

('fig', 'figures/fig1_function_method_map.png', 'Fig. 1 The classified subset mapped across battery-management functions and method families (88 primary-application papers; see Section 2.5 for terminology). Cell values are paper counts. The 15 review-labelled papers (12 method-family reviews plus 3 review-type syntheses coded under their dominant method family, two of them physics-informed) are excluded here; the Reviews column of Table 3 states the reconciliation directly, so the physics-informed family comprises 22 primary papers plus 2 review-type syntheses (24 family-coded; Table 2 gives the primary counts).'),

('h1', '2. Review Methodology'),

('h2', '2.1 Scope and objective'),
('p',
 "The objective of this review is to characterize the current state and recent evolution of machine learning applied to battery management, understood as the set of estimation, protection, and control functions performed by or in support of a battery management system. The scope covers SOC estimation, SOH estimation and capacity tracking, remaining useful life (RUL) prediction and early-life prognosis, charging strategy optimization, thermal-state estimation and battery thermal management, fault diagnosis, anomaly detection and safety prediction, and cross-cutting methodological themes (physics-informed learning, transfer learning, interpretability, digital twins, cloud and fleet management, and foundation models). Battery *design* and *materials-discovery* applications of machine learning fall outside the scope except where they directly support a management function, such as manufacturing-quality prediction of cell capacity."),

('h2', '2.2 Literature search strategy'),
('p',
 "All candidate records were retrieved programmatically from the Crossref REST API between 4 and 5 September 2026 using 30 bibliographic query strings spanning the application areas listed above (for example, \u201cstate of charge estimation lithium-ion battery machine learning\u201d, \u201cremaining useful life prediction battery deep learning\u201d, \u201cphysics-informed neural network lithium-ion battery\u201d, and \u201clarge language model battery\u201d). Queries were issued in five passes: a top-cited pass over 2020\u20132026 sorted by citation count; a relevance-sorted recency pass restricted to mid-2024 onward; two dedicated relevance-sorted passes restricted to 2020\u20132022 and to 2023; and a mid-window 2021\u20132023 coverage pass, which together yielded 3,000 retrieved records. After DOI-based deduplication \u2014 1,450 duplicates removed \u2014 1,550 unique records remained. Six landmark studies \u2014 including the early-life prediction study [[10.1038/s41560-019-0356-8]] and the prognostics review [[10.1016/j.joule.2019.11.018]] \u2014 were additionally safeguarded as forced seeds: five were retrieved and verified individually because citation-sorted retrieval had crowded them out, and one was already among the top-cited hits."),

('p',
 "Two properties of this search design should be stated plainly. First, the Crossref registry is a metadata registry, not a discovery engine of the kind Scopus or Web of Science provide; it was chosen deliberately, because the aim of this review is that every included record be individually verifiable by direct DOI resolution, which registry-native retrieval guarantees and secondary databases cannot. The cost is a recall floor: coverage depends on Crossref registration, and the usual cross-database triangulation is absent. Second, the top-cited pass inherits the biases of citation counts \u2014 it favors established, English-language, high-profile work and mechanically over-weights older publications. The three remaining passes exist to counter exactly these biases, and all trend statements in this paper are statements about trends within this citation-weighted, English-language, journal-article sample rather than about the field in the aggregate."),

('h2', '2.3 Screening and selection procedure'),
('p',
 "The screening funnel is shown in Fig. 2. From 1,550 deduplicated records, a title-level relevance gate requiring both battery-related and machine-learning-related terminology retained 883 records and excluded 667 (predominantly adjacent-field papers \u2014 general machine-learning methods, adjacent-domain bibliometrics, and battery electrochemistry without a learning component \u2014 that surfaced through loose bibliographic matching). Venue and document-type screening then removed 84 records \u2014 conference abstracts and proceedings series (e.g., ECS Meeting Abstracts, IET/IFAC proceedings), preprint servers (SSRN), and non-scholarly venues \u2014 in line with the decision to restrict the corpus to peer-reviewed journal articles, leaving 799 eligible records. A stratified selection step then drew 107 candidate articles from these, balancing the eight management functions and publication years. Within each function stratum the draw was deterministic rather than random: records were ranked by Crossref citation count and taken from the top of that ranking, with the ranking walked deeper specifically to cover recent publication years, which citation sorting alone would have starved. Six landmark seed studies were safeguarded at this point (one already captured by the searches, five retrieved individually), giving 112 candidates. Full-text curation excluded 19 records \u2014 corrigenda, misindexed records, scope mismatches (for example, vehicle-level lifecycle assessment or charging-infrastructure demand modeling without a BMS function), and quota-driven trimming of over-represented function clusters \u2014 while 10 targeted additions were made from the screened pool for under-represented themes, a net change of \u22129 that yields the final classified corpus of 103 studies \u2014 98 drawn from the eligible pool plus the 5 individually retrieved seeds. The 799 eligible records themselves are retained as a rule-based Tier-1 pool, released in full with per-record screening provenance, and serving two further purposes in this paper: pool-level function-tag baselines that are free of the subset\u2019s quotas (Section 3.3), and a documented boundary on what the subset can support. Readers should hold the implication of the design plainly in view: function and year distributions inside the 103 partly reflect the quota and citation-ranking choices made here, not only the field\u2019s shape, so all such distributions are reported as corpus composition, and the quota-free pool measurements are stated wherever they exist."),

('fig', 'figures/fig2_prisma.png', 'Fig. 2 Study-selection flowchart. Records were retrieved from the Crossref REST API on 4\u20135 September 2026, deduplicated, screened at title level for battery and machine-learning relevance, screened at venue and document-type level, and curated to the final 103-study corpus. Every included record\u2019s metadata was verified by direct DOI resolution against the Crossref registry.'),

('h2', '2.4 Inclusion and exclusion criteria'),
('p',
 "Table 1 summarizes the criteria. Inclusion required: (i) a peer-reviewed journal article; (ii) a battery or battery-system subject; (iii) machine learning as a substantive component of the contribution, whether estimation, control, diagnosis, or analysis; (iv) a publication year between 2019 and 2026, with 2019 admitted only for the field-defining early-life prediction study; and (v) English language. Exclusion criteria were: review-only papers without a primary application (admitted only into the cross-cutting cluster, capped at 12 studies; three further review-type syntheses \u2014 two physics-informed, one classical \u2014 are coded under their dominant method family, so the corpus carries 15 review-labelled papers in total, per the terminology of Section 2.5), pure materials-discovery or cell-design papers, charging-infrastructure or grid-demand studies without an onboard management function, preprints and conference records, and duplicate-venue or misindexed records."),

('tbl', 'table1'),

('h2', '2.5 Classification framework'),
('p',
 "Each corpus paper was classified along six fields: (1) *management function*, with eight values \u2014 state estimation (SOC), health estimation (SOH), prognostics (RUL), charging control, thermal management, fault and safety, cell balancing and equivalent-circuit modeling, and a cross-cutting cluster for methodological and system-level themes; (2) *method family*, with values including classical ML (Gaussian process regression, support vector machines, random forests, shallow networks), deep learning (recurrent, convolutional, attention and graph architectures), physics-informed and hybrid model\u2013data learning, optimization-augmented learning (Bayesian optimization, surrogate-assisted and meta-heuristic schemes), reinforcement and control learning, transfer and domain adaptation, and foundation models/LLMs; (3) *chemistry* (NMC, LFP, LCO, lithium-metal, lithium\u2013sulfur, mixed, or unspecified); (4) *primary data source* (own laboratory cycling, public datasets, laboratory impedance spectroscopy, real vehicle or fleet data, simulation, or multiple sources); (5) *validation approach* (within-cell splits, cross-cell holdout, cross-chemistry or cross-domain transfer protocols, online/real-vehicle demonstration, experimental hardware, or simulation); and (6) a short coded summary of the headline finding and the principal limitation. The validation categories of field (5) are aggregated into three stringency tiers used throughout the paper. Under the *protocol-only definition*, a *strong* protocol is one whose design isolates the generalization claim: cross-cell holdout, cross-chemistry holdout, cross-domain holdout, and online or real-vehicle demonstration. *Weak* splits leak information between train and test: within-cell splits and k-fold cross-validation without cell-level isolation. The remaining classes are *environment-only*: experimental-hardware, simulation, and single-system case-study demonstrations, which evidence an operating environment but not a data-isolation protocol; the *broader definition* of stringency additionally counts experimental-hardware demonstrations as strong. Every strong-protocol count in Sections 3 and 9 states which definition it uses, and review-labelled studies carry no primary validation. Classification was performed by the lead author with conservative coding from full texts: where a paper\u2019s specifics could not be established confidently, broader categories were assigned. Three terms are used consistently hereafter: a *corpus paper* is any of the 103 included studies; a *primary study* (or *primary-application paper*) is one of the 88 whose contribution is a primary application rather than a review; and the 15 review-labelled papers (12 method-family reviews plus 3 review-type syntheses coded under their dominant method family) are identified by the method-family and validation fields and are excluded from Fig. 1. Papers are assigned a single dominant value per field even where several apply. To probe the reliability of that coding, two co-author coders (S.S.I.A. and J.I.C., the second and third authors) later blind-re-coded a 30-paper stratified random sample (drawn by function and year band, seed-pinned) under the released codebook. The blinding was procedural rather than external: the coders are authors of this study; they coded without access to the released labels or to each other’s sheets, from sheets locked and hash-tracked at completion, with a filed blinding memo documenting what each coder had opened before coding; but they are not independent of the paper, and their later author-contribution roles included verification of classifications and counts. Their access was uneven: coder B worked from 6 full texts plus 1 preprint (23 of 30 papers from abstracts only) and coder C from 2 full texts (28 from abstracts only), so the study measures abstract-level reproducibility of the coding, not full-text agreement. With those scopes stated, agreement passes the κ ≥ 0.66 threshold on management function (κ = 0.92, bootstrap 95% CI 0.80–1.00), method family (0.88, CI 0.73–1.00), and primary data source (0.88, CI 0.72–1.00), and falls below it on chemistry (0.55, CI 0.16–0.83) and validation protocol (0.48, CI 0.31–0.65) — the two fields least often stated at abstract level. On the count-bearing strong-versus-non-strong bucket the two coders agree on 25 of 30 papers (raw agreement 83%, κ = 0.52, CI 0.09–0.84). Against the released labels their bucket kappas are 0.22 and 0.42, with the conservative weak-code fallback for unreported protocols — declared in advance by both coders — driving most of that gap. No full-text dual coding was performed, so the strong/weak counts of Section 3 rest on a single full-text coding by the lead author, with two quantitative buffers: the label-noise sensitivity analysis that propagates the observed coder-versus-label disagreement into the headline counts (Section 3.4), and the complete release of per-paper labels, coder sheets, and the itemized disagreements, so that any reader can re-run, check, correct, or extend the coding — and so that a formal full-text reliability measurement can be run on the released labels."),

('h2', '2.6 Comparative synthesis approach'),
('p',
 "The synthesis proceeds on two tracks. Quantitatively, the classification fields are aggregated into cross-tabulations \u2014 function by year, method family by year, chemistry, data source, and validation practice \u2014 that characterize the corpus as a sample of the field\u2019s recent output (Section 3); every figure and every count in Section 3 was generated programmatically from the released dataset rather than compiled by hand; the full pipeline is publicly available in the repository accompanying this paper. Qualitatively, the review synthesizes each function\u2019s literature critically: what each cluster of methods assumes, what it demonstrates, and where its evidentiary support is thin (Sections 4\u20138). Cross-referencing the two tracks then yields the gap analysis of Section 9."),

('p',
 "The methodology has limitations that readers should weigh. The corpus is drawn from Crossref-registered journal articles and therefore inherits that registry\u2019s coverage: influential conference papers (a significant venue for control-oriented BMS work) are excluded by design, and very recent articles registered with incomplete metadata may evade relevance screening. Title-level relevance screening can miss relevant papers whose titles avoid standard terminology, and citation-count ranking privileges English-language, high-profile venues. Classification reliability is documented on a 30-paper blind re-coding subset rather than measured corpus-wide (three of five fields above threshold, two below, with sensitivity ranges reported; the re-coding was abstract-level and co-author-coded, per Section 2.5). The classified corpus is additionally quota-curated rather than sampled: its function, year, and method distributions are conditioned on the stratified draw of Section 2.3, and the eligible pool from which it was drawn has its own retrieval-design shaping (unequal query coverage across years, Section 2.2), so neither level supports field-output trend measurement; distributional statements in Section 3 are therefore framed as corpus composition, with quota-free pool-level baselines stated where they exist. These limitations qualify the quantitative aggregates of Section 3, which should be read as a structured sample of the field\u2019s output rather than a census \u2014 and they are the reason this paper describes itself as a scoping review with bibliometric classification rather than as a meta-analysis."),

('h1', '3. Corpus Overview and Five-Year Evolution'),

('h2', '3.1 Publication trend'),
('p',
 "The corpus spans 2019\u20132026 with the following composition: 9 papers in 2020, 13 in 2021, 13 in 2022, 22 in 2023, 20 in 2024, 18 in 2025, and 7 in the partial year 2026 (retrieval date September 2026), plus the 2019 seed (Fig. 3). This profile should be read as the output of the sampling design, not as a measurement of field output. Three design choices shape it. First, the stratified draw of Section 2.3 balanced publication years by construction, so year-to-year differences inside the subset are muted by design rather than observed. Second, the citation-ranked within-stratum rule preferentially captures highly cited work. Third, the eligible pool itself has a retrieval-shaped year mix \u2014 the five query passes of Section 2.2 covered year windows unequally (a dedicated 2023 pass, a recency pass from mid-2024, older passes ending 2022), so the pool\u2019s own year profile (55, 85, 139, 223, 56, 126, and 114 records for 2020\u20132026, plus one record Crossref-stamped 2027) measures query coverage as much as publication. Neither level supports a claim that field output rose, fell, or accelerated; what the composition does show, within those limits, is a broadening from single-function estimators toward cross-cutting system-level contributions in the recent quotas."),

('p',
 "Citation weight tells a complementary story about influence. Citation counts throughout this paper are the Crossref registry\u2019s is-referenced-by-count values retrieved on 5 September 2026 and are included per paper in the released dataset; they are typically lower than Google Scholar counts for the same paper and should be compared only against themselves. The corpus accumulates roughly 18,400 recorded citations, but the distribution is steeply skewed: the median paper has 65 citations, while the 2019 seed alone holds 3,002 and the two 2020 landmark studies (Battery Lifetime Prognostics [[10.1016/j.joule.2019.11.018]] and closed-loop fast charging [[10.1038/s41586-020-1994-5]]) hold 1,239 and 1,078 respectively. Median citations fall monotonically with publication year \u2014 from 441 (2020) through 141 (2022) and 46 (2024) to single digits for 2026 publications \u2014 which is partly an age effect and partly evidence that the field\u2019s attention concentrates on a small set of canonical results. For a scoping review this skew matters: narrative reviews that emphasize highly cited work systematically describe the field as it stood in 2020\u20132021, whereas the recent relevance-sorted portions of this corpus capture the current working frontier that citations have not yet caught up with."),

('fig', 'figures/fig2_year_trend.png', 'Fig. 3 Corpus composition by publication year (bars, left axis) with cumulative total (line, right axis). The 2019 seed study is included in the cumulative count. Years were balanced by the sampling design of Section 2.3, so the profile is corpus composition, not a field-output trend (Section 3.1).'),

('h2', '3.2 Method-family evolution'),
('p',
 "The defining meta-finding of this review is the compositional shift in method families across the corpus (Fig. 4, Table 2) \u2014 a within-corpus observation under the quota design of Section 2.3, not a field-level growth measurement. Classical ML \u2014 Gaussian process regression, support vector machines, ensemble regressors, and shallow networks on engineered features \u2014 dominates the early corpus: 12 of the 23 papers published from 2019 through 2021. Deep sequence models (LSTM, GRU, CNN, attention and graph architectures) rise through 2022\u20132023 and remain the largest single family for prediction tasks thereafter. Physics-informed and hybrid model\u2013data learning \u2014 including physics-informed neural networks (PINNs), physics-constrained autoencoders, filter-embedded networks, and multiphysics-ML fusion \u2014 emerges in 2021\u20132022 and grows steadily: counted year by year, the family contributes 1, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, and 5 primary papers in 2020\u20132026 \u2014 the only family with a monotonically non-decreasing primary trajectory within the subset, with 2026 a partial year at the September 2026 retrieval \u2014 so that 13 of its 22 primary papers fall in 2024\u20132026 and it is the largest single family among the 7 papers published in 2026 (24 family-coded papers including two review-type syntheses; Table 2 gives the primary totals; Fig. 4 the yearly counts). Optimization-augmented learning (Bayesian optimization for protocol design, surrogate-assisted multi-objective schemes, and meta-heuristic-tuned networks) peaks in 2024 (5 of its 11 papers). Transfer and domain-adaptation approaches appear consistently from 2022 and accelerate in 2024\u20132025 (3 of its 5 papers), reinforcement learning remains scarce but visible (1 primary paper, with RL embedded in further studies), and the first foundation-model and LLM-based studies appear in 2025\u20132026 (2 papers) [[10.1016/j.ress.2026.112416]][[10.1016/j.ensm.2026.104983]]."),

('tbl', 'TABLE_METH'),
('p',
 "Within-family dynamics deserve comment. The persistent classical-ML share is not inertia: Gaussian process regression survives because it natively returns calibrated uncertainty \u2014 the one property certification-minded applications cannot do without \u2014 and because feature-based estimators remain the cheapest to embed. The deep-learning share consolidated around recurrent and attention architectures for trajectory data and convolutional architectures for curve data, with graph networks appearing where cell-to-cell or feature-to-feature relations matter. The optimization-augmented family grew along two distinct branches: meta-heuristically tuned networks (PSO, GA, and swarm variants), which improve accuracy at a documented training cost, and Bayesian or surrogate-assisted optimization of *operating protocols*, which changes what can be experimentally discovered rather than merely what can be predicted. Conflating these two branches, as broad-brush surveys tend to do, obscures the more consequential of the two developments."),

('fig', 'figures/fig3_method_evolution.png', 'Fig. 4 Method-family composition of the classified subset by publication year (paper counts; reviews shown as their own band). Yearly totals match the corpus totals of Table 3. Composition of the curated subset under the Section 2.3 quota design; not a field-level trend measurement (Section 3.2).'),

('p',
 "Three observations qualify this trajectory. First, the families are not mutually exclusive in practice \u2014 a PSO-tuned LSTM is simultaneously deep learning and optimization-augmented learning \u2014 and the corpus classification assigns each paper to its dominant family; the composition therefore understates method mixing. Second, the rise of physics-informed learning is partly a response to the recognized brittleness of purely data-driven models under distribution shift, but physics-informed papers in the corpus are, on average, validated no more stringently than others \u2014 if anything somewhat less stringently, a point quantified in Section 3.4. Third, the foundation-model cluster is too small to represent a trend; its significance is that LLM-based management (fleet risk analysis and LLM-based health estimation, Section 8.4) is appearing in mainstream energy journals at all. A pool-level cross-check bounds these observations: among the 799 eligible records, method-theme query tags show no monotonic physics-informed trend once the retrieval windows\u2019 unequal year coverage is accounted for, so the family trajectories of this section describe the curated subset alone."),

('h2', '3.3 Application-area distribution'),
('p',
 "Functionally, the corpus is dominated by the health-prognostics complex: health estimation (20 papers) and remaining-useful-life prognostics (15) together account for 34% of the 103-paper corpus, followed by the cross-cutting cluster (24, including reviews, physics-informed methods, interpretability, transfer learning, digital twins, cloud/fleet themes, and foundation models), state estimation (17), fault and safety (9), charging control (8), thermal management (8), and cell balancing/ECM (2) (Fig. 5). Fig. 5 matches the all-corpus totals of Table 3 (103 papers); Fig. 1, by contrast, is drawn over the 88-paper primary-application corpus and excludes the 15 review-labelled papers, with the per-function reconciliation stated in that figure\u2019s caption and in Table 3\u2019s Reviews column. Because these shares are conditioned on the sampling quotas (Section 2.3), the quota-free eligible pool provides the check. Its query-theme tags — which reproduce the subset’s full-text primary-function label for 53 of the 75 core-area papers found in the pool (71%, counting a hit when any of a record’s tags matches; Section 2.5) — give health estimation 158, state estimation 133, prognostics 103, thermal management 99, fault and safety 96, charging control 64, and cell balancing 38 of 799 records. The pool confirms the same ordering at the top: the health-prognostics complex dominates, consistent with the commercial pull of lifetime prediction for warranties and second-life markets. It also confirms the same floor — balancing is the smallest core function at pool level as well as in the subset, so its thin representation is a property of the retrieved literature rather than an artifact of the quotas, and ML has so far contributed mainly at the level of pack-level insight rather than as a balancing controller \u2014 the corpus\u2019s most-cited balancing-related result is the demonstration that routine balancing can be dispensed with in well-managed packs, reducing hazardous lithium-plating side reactions [[10.1016/j.est.2023.106931]]. Thermal management, by contrast, is not scarce at pool level (99 tagged records, comparable to fault and safety): the subset’s small thermal count reflects the coding scheme — thermal-runaway papers are coded under fault and safety — and the curation quotas, not field scarcity; the genuine thermal deficit is evidential rather than topical, since no subset paper yet closes the thermal control loop on hardware (Section 7.1)."),

('tbl', 'TABLE2'),
('p',
 "Chemistry and venue concentration complete the corpus profile. NMC-type cells anchor 54 of the 103 studies and LFP 15; the remainder split across LCO, lithium-metal, lithium\u2013sulfur, mixed panels, and unspecified chemistries \u2014 a distribution that mirrors EV market share but leaves sodium-ion, solid-state, and high-temperature systems nearly absent from the ML-management literature. Venue concentration is equally marked: the Journal of Energy Storage (15 papers), Energy (13), and Applied Energy (9) head the corpus, with Journal of Power Sources, Energy Storage Materials, and Reliability Engineering & System Safety following; IEEE venues account for 7 of the 103 papers, three of them in IEEE Transactions on Transportation Electrification. The pool shows the same shape — Journal of Energy Storage 103, Energy 61, and Batteries 45 of 799 records, with IEEE-prefixed venues at 9.0% — so for readers the practical consequence is that the ML-BMS literature’s center of gravity sits in energy-systems journals rather than in machine-learning venues: partly a statement about where this work is reviewed and indexed, and partly a Crossref-registry artifact (Section 2.2), rather than evidence about how impact is achieved in this field."),

('fig', 'figures/fig4_area_distribution.png', 'Fig. 5 Application-area distribution of the corpus. The cross-cutting cluster includes reviews, physics-informed learning, interpretability, transfer learning, digital twins, cloud and fleet management, and foundation models.'),

('h2', '3.4 Data landscape and validation practice'),
('p',
 "The data underpinning the corpus are strikingly concentrated (Fig. 6, Table 4). Thirty-six papers rely primarily on laboratory cycling of the authors\u2019 own cells; the public Severson/Toyota-MIT\u2013Stanford (MATR) cycle-life dataset alone supports 11 papers, NASA and CALCE datasets support 6 each, and laboratory impedance spectroscopy 6; 10 papers use real vehicle or fleet data as their primary data source \u2014 a distinct measure from the 8 studies whose *validation* is an online or real-vehicle demonstration; 12 are simulation-based; and 15 are reviews or perspectives that synthesize rather than generate data. Chemistry reporting is similarly skewed: 54 papers work on NMC-based cells, 15 on LFP, and the remainder on LCO, lithium-metal, lithium\u2013sulfur, mixed panels, or unspecified chemistries."),

('p',
 "Validation practice \u2014 arguably the field\u2019s most consequential weakness \u2014 is summarized in Fig. 6 (right panel). Twenty-four papers validate on cross-cell holdout protocols, the most defensible laboratory standard; but 12 papers use within-cell splits (training and testing on interleaved segments of the same cell\u2019s life), 5 use k-fold cross-validation without cross-cell isolation, and 15 are reviews without primary validation. Only 8 studies validate a system online or on real vehicle/fleet data, and only 7 validate across chemistries. Because fleet-data *use* and online *validation* are different measures, Table 6 cross-tabulates them: of the 10 papers whose primary data are real-vehicle or fleet streams, 8 are online-validated and 2 are validated by cross-domain holdout or case-study protocols instead (a laboratory-data paper whose online claim was a desktop demonstration was recoded to experimental hardware after full-text re-check). This distribution quantifies a widely acknowledged but rarely measured problem: the majority of published accuracy claims are established under laboratory regimes that differ from deployment in chemistry coverage, temperature envelope, current-profile realism, and sensor quality \u2014 a gap that recurs throughout Sections 4\u20138."),

('fig', 'figures/fig5_data_validation.png', 'Fig. 6 Primary data sources (left) and validation approaches (right) across the corpus. Public-dataset reliance concentrates on the Severson/Toyota (MATR), NASA, and CALCE benchmarks; online or real-vehicle validation remains rare.'),

('tbl', 'TABLE_DATASETS'),
('p',
 "Validation practice deserves its own cross-tabulation because it moderates every accuracy claim in the corpus (Table 5). Cross-cell holdout \u2014 training on some cells and testing on unseen siblings \u2014 is the strongest widely used laboratory protocol (24 papers), and cross-chemistry or cross-domain transfer studies make the strictest demands (10 papers). At the weaker end, within-cell splits (12 papers) and unsupervised k-fold cross-validation (5 papers) leak trajectory information between train and test sets, producing optimistic errors that do not survive cell-to-cell variability; the review literature has long warned of this, and the corpus quantifies the warning. Experimental-hardware demonstrations (20 papers) and online or real-vehicle demonstrations (8 papers) anchor the deployment end of the spectrum, and it is these \u2014 not the largest architectures \u2014 that carry the field\u2019s deployment evidence. Crossing validation practice with method family substantiates Section 3.2\u2019s remark with numbers: 8 of the 22 physics-informed/hybrid primary papers (36%) use a strong protocol, against 34 of the 66 other primary papers (52%) \u2014 Fisher\u2019s exact p = 0.32, odds ratio 0.54 (95% CI 0.21\u20131.46); under the broader definition of stringency (Section 2.5) that also counts experimental-hardware demonstrations, the contrast is 13 of 22 (59%) versus 49 of 66 (74%), p = 0.19. Neither difference is statistically significant. To quantify how much these counts depend on the single-coder labels, a label-noise scenario analysis perturbs them directly. In each of 4,000 seeded Monte Carlo draws (seed 7), a label-flip rate is drawn from a Normal distribution centered on the observed coder-versus-label bucket error rate of the reliability study (0.40 and 0.30 for the two coders; mean 0.35) with standard deviation 0.02, truncated at zero; the drawn rate then flips strong/non-strong bucket labels at random across the 88 primary papers, and both the overall strong share and the physics-versus-other Fisher exact test are recomputed. The strong-protocol count spans 35\u201352 of the 88 primary papers (39.8\u201359.1%), and the family contrast remains non-significant in 94.9% of draws \u2014 a physics prior does not, in itself, demonstrably buy validation rigor."),

('tbl', 'TABLE_VALID'),
('tbl', 'TABLE_FLEET'),
('p',
 "Reading Fig. 6 and Tables 4 and 5 together yields the section\u2019s headline: the field\u2019s data supply is narrow, and its validation habits are calibrated to that narrowness. Public benchmarks made cumulative progress possible \u2014 the Severson dataset alone catalyzed a family of early-prediction methods \u2014 but they now also cap the credibility of reported generalization. The studies that escape this trap are precisely those that treat data sourcing and validation design as the research contribution: the multi-cell pipeline studies, the fleet-data health estimators, and the transfer-learning frameworks."),

('p',
 "The concentration can be stated as a metric. Across 2019\u20132026, 23 primary papers \u2014 26% of the 88 primary-application papers \u2014 rest on the same three public benchmarks (MATR, NASA, CALCE; Fig. 7). These benchmarks together contain on the order of a few hundred cells, while 36 further papers rest on private laboratory data that are rarely released: the empirical base is therefore not one benchmark monoculture but two poles \u2014 a small set of shared benchmarks onto which comparative claims crowd, and many unshared laboratory datasets over which accuracy claims cannot be audited at all. Output rose from single digits per year in 2019\u20132020 to 18\u201322 papers per year in 2023\u20132025, but benchmark reuse itself fluctuates between 1 and 7 papers per year with no trend, so no saturation dynamic is claimed; the structural finding is this two-pole data fragmentation, which inflates apparent comparability on the shared pole and precludes reproducibility on the private one."),

('fig', 'figures/fig7_dataset_saturation.png', 'Fig. 7 Benchmark reuse versus classified-subset output. Papers in the classified subset using the three dominant public benchmarks per year (stacked bars, left axis) against classified-subset papers per year (line, right axis). Both series describe the curated 103-study subset, not the eligible corpus; benchmark reuse fluctuates year to year rather than tracking output.'),

('h2', '3.5 Sensitivity analysis: does the journal-only corpus understate validation practice?'),

('p',
 "Because the corpus excludes conference proceedings \u2014 a major venue for control-oriented BMS work (CDC, ACC, IFAC, VPPC, ITEC) \u2014 the validation findings of Section 3.4 could in principle be an artifact of venue selection: if conference papers systematically demonstrate more hardware and hardware-in-the-loop validation than journals, the journal-only sample would understate the field\u2019s actual practice. To test this, we curated a sensitivity set of 14 battery papers from six control and transportation-electrification venues (ACC, ECC, CCDC, APEC, VPPC, ITEC, 2019\u20132025), retrieved by venue-scoped Crossref queries, restricted to proceedings articles, ranked by citation count, and verified by the same DOI-resolution procedure as the main corpus (released in full as conference_sensitivity.csv). A pre-stated inclusion rule was then applied mechanically to all retrieved proceedings papers (Table 7 lists all 14 with the rule's outcome): a paper enters the learning-path comparison only if machine learning is a substantive component of its contribution. Under this rule 8 of the 14 qualify; the remainder are conventional model-based estimators (Kalman-filter variants, multiple-model adaptive estimation, batch least squares), an EKF-critique, and one overview, and all 14 are released so that the rule can be audited rather than trusted. Validation practice was classified conservatively from the indexed abstracts into four classes: hardware/experimental stated, public dataset, simulation-level, and not stated."),

('tbl', 'TABLE_CONF'),

('p',
 "The result does not indicate a venue gap in stated validation evidence. Of the 8 learning-based conference papers, 2 (25%) state hardware or experimental validation in their indexed abstracts, 2 rest on public datasets, 1 describes simulation-level validation, and 3 state no validation basis at all \u2014 against 20 of the 88 primary-application journal papers (23%) with experimental-hardware demonstrations. Indexed abstract-level transparency about validation is, if anything, lower on the conference path. Two caveats bound this finding: conference abstracts are frequently truncated in bibliographic indexes, so the comparison measures stated evidence rather than underlying practice; and the sensitivity set is a curated sample, not a census. Within those bounds, however, the journal-only corpus does not understate the field\u2019s validation transparency \u2014 and the observation that neither venue type reports validation systematically strengthens, rather than weakens, the case for the reporting standards proposed in Section 9.7."),
]